<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="cyclic electron flow" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>The |FRAME: PWY-101 &quot;aerobic photosynthesis pathway&quot;| generates two main products: proton motive force (which is translated to ATP production by ATP synthase) and NADPH. When the ATP supply drops and the level of NADPH rises, plant chloroplasts and cyanobacteria can shift to an alternative pathway that bypasses photosystem II, called &quot;cyclic electron flow&quot;. In this pathway the electrons generated by |FRAME: EC-1.97.1.12 &quot;photosystem I&quot;| are transferred to ferredoxin, just like they are in regular photosynthesis. However, instead of transferring those electrons to |FRAME: EC-1.18.1.2|, which results in NADPH production, the ferredoxin transfers the electrons to the NDH-1 complex, which returns them to the |FRAME: PLASTOQUINONE plastoquinone| pool. Plastoquinol delivers the elelctrons to |FRAME: EC-7.1.1.6| (known better as the cytochrome &lt;i&gt;b&lt;sub&gt;6&lt;/sub&gt;f&lt;/i&gt; complex), which generates proton motive force due to the Q-cycle and transfers the electrons to |FRAME: Plastocyanins plastocyanin|. Plastocyanin delivers the electron back to |FRAME: EC-1.97.1.12 &quot;photosystem I&quot;|, closing the cycle. The truncated cycle bypasses NADPH production, but generates ATP due to the proton motive force generated by |FRAME: EC-7.1.1.6|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="chawkins" />
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
